.TH BX 1 "2026-10-29" "bx 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bx \- inspect ELF64 binaries
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bx
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.I file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bx
is a small, static ELF64 binary explorer written in x86-64 assembly.
It uses raw Linux system calls and has no libc or dynamic loader dependency.
It is intended for the first look at an unfamiliar executable, not as a
replacement for
.BR readelf (1)
or
.BR objdump (1).
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info \fIfile\fR
Print the file size, ELF type, machine, entry point, and section and segment
counts.
.TP
.B sections \fIfile\fR
Print the section table: name, type, flags, address, file offset, and size.
Section names are sanitized; longer names are shortened with a trailing
.B ..
.TP
.B hex \fR[\fIoptions\fR] \fIfile\fR
Dump bytes with an ASCII column.
.TP
.B help
Print usage information.
.TP
.B version
Print the version.
.SH OPTIONS
The
.B info
and
.B sections
commands accept an optional
.B --
before or after the file name.
The
.B hex
command accepts the following options:
.TP
.BI \-o " " offset ", " \-\-offset " " offset
Start at
.IR offset .
Decimal and
.B 0x
prefixed hexadecimal values are accepted.
.TP
.BI \-n " " length ", " \-\-length " " length
Dump at most
.I length
bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing. The next argument is treated as a file name even if it
begins with a hyphen.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be opened, read, or parsed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.SH LIMITS
ELF64 little-endian files are supported. 32-bit and big-endian ELF files are
rejected. Only regular files are accepted; devices, FIFOs, sockets and
zero-size pseudo-files are rejected. Standard input is not supported.
.PP
Segments are counted but not listed. JSON output, color, PE/COFF and
disassembly are not implemented.
.SH AUTHOR
Lazar Avramovic
.PP
https://github.com/Lazar-Avramovic
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR readelf (1),
.BR objdump (1),
.BR xxd (1)
